{
  "id": "COMPOUND:17374 - PROTEIN:4928",
  "verbalization": "Mollicellin N is implicated in the interaction with T-cell surface glycoprotein CD3 through multiple pathways, with the core mechanism involving the targeting of A704, which is a common intermediate in both pathways. The evidence suggests that Mollicellin N acts as a target for two distinct compounds, one of which (palmarumycin CP18) directly binds to CD3, while the other (3Beta-Hydroxyolean-12-En-23,29-Dioic Acid) indirectly influences CD3 through A704. This indicates that Mollicellin N may modulate CD3 through different molecular mechanisms, potentially affecting T-cell function. The integration of these pathways highlights a possible role for Mollicellin N in regulating T-cell signaling, though the exact mechanism of action remains to be fully elucidated. The evidence supports a biologically plausible link between Mollicellin N and CD3, but further experimental validation is needed to confirm the specific mode of action and therapeutic potential.",
